{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "The apertium-recursive module was developed to deal with the limitations of finite-state chunking. It works by applying structural transfer rules recursively using context-free grammars (CFGs) and a Generalized Left-right Right-reduce (GLR) parser. This allows the processing of nested structures such as relative clauses or prepositional phrases.",
  "citations": [
    {"ref": 2, "quote": "To deal with the limitations of finite-state chunking, the apertium-recursive module (Swanson et al. 2021) was developed by Daniel Swanson as part of Google Summer of Code 20193 to apply structural transfer rules recursively using contextfree grammars (CFGs) and a Generalized Left-right Right-reduce (GLR) parser."}
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}

