{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "Gutless says the timing of a water preload should depend on your age because gastric emptying, which refers to the rate at which food and fluid leaves your stomach, tends to slow down as you get older. This means that a 30-minute water preload doesn't seem to work as well in younger compared to older individuals [2].",
  "citations": [
    {
      "ref": 2,
      "quote": "A 30-minute water preload doesn’t seem to work as well in younger compared to older individuals, which may be down to differences in the rate at which water leaves your stomach."
    }
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}

{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "The baseline TatarTTS models were built and trained using the TatarTTS dataset, which consists of 70 hours of transcribed speech from two speakers (one male and one female) [1]. The models were trained on 30 hours of speech data per speaker, with each model undergoing training for 1,000 epochs on an NVIDIA DGX server using A100 GPUs [4]. The training process involved the random initialization of VITS models for both speakers [2].",
  "citations": [
    {"ref": 1, "quote": "This study presents the TatarTTS corpus, which consists of 70 hours of transcribed speech from two speakers (one male and one female). Furthermore, we have created preliminary TTS models as a baseline for the Tatar language."}, 
    {"ref": 2, "quote": "Our methodology involved the random initialization of VITS models for both female and male speakers. Subsequently, these models underwent training on the given dataset for 1,000 epochs per speaker."}, 
    {"ref": 4, "quote": "We trained separate TTS models for each speaker, creating single-speaker models. Each model was trained on A100 graphics processing units (GPUs) on an NVIDIA DGX server."}
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}

{
  "status": "answer",
  "answer": "Grapes play a significant role in Gutless, both in explaining energy density and in the author's diet. In the context of energy density, grapes are used as an example to illustrate how lower energy density foods can keep you fuller for longer [2]. In the author's diet, frozen grapes are used as a dessert alternative that satisfies the sweet tooth without causing excessive calorie intake [3].",
  "citations": [
    {"ref": 2, "quote": "If you wanted to eat 150 calories of raisins, you’d be able to eat approximately 50 grams. Yet the same number of calories would give you almost 220 grams of grapes — more than four times as much."}, 
    {"ref": 3, "quote": "Frozen grapes have an energy density of just 0.7. This compares favorably to a tub of Ben & Jerry’s Peanut Butter Cup, which has an energy density of 3.4."}
  ],
  "clarification": ""
}
